Shared cleanup admission can starve background cleanup
Link: https://chatgpt.com/codex/cloud/security/findings/d2d92c75a018819188e4df57d4ff57ae?sev=&repo=https%3A%2F%2Fgithub.com%2Fjustincormack%2Fargmin
Criticality: medium (attack path: medium)
Status: fixed

# Resolution
Fixed in commit [`cd33dda2`](https://github.com/justincormack/argmin/commit/cd33dda2) (`Split background cleanup admission lanes`).

The fix removes the single shared `DurableCleanup` fairness domain. Background cleanup admission is now split into separate fixed nonzero lanes: `ReclaimCleanup` for object payload reclaim and bucket-delete finalization, `LifecycleCleanup` for lifecycle sweeps, and `StreamSessionCleanup` for abandoned stream-session cleanup. These workers still use the shared admission framework and non-blocking permits, but unrelated cleanup categories no longer contend for the same single counter.

The UAT metrics summary now reports the three cleanup classes independently, so reclaim, lifecycle, and stream-session admission/denial/finish behavior can be observed separately.

Verification for the fix:
