# Summary
Introduced: the commit changes runtime behavior, not just telemetry, by adding a shared fixed-size admission gate for DurableCleanup. Because unrelated cleanup workers now contend for a single non-fair permit, one cleanup workload can starve or significantly delay another.
The new background admission framework sets the DurableCleanup concurrency limit to 1 and wires both object/bucket reclaim and lifecycle sweeping through that same class. Admission is a non-blocking try_acquire with no fairness or queueing. If lifecycle cleanup holds the permit during a long full-bucket sweep, reclaim work is repeatedly deferred; conversely, a continuous reclaim backlog can cause lifecycle sweeps to miss admission and then sleep until the next lifecycle interval. An authenticated client able to generate large lifecycle scans or sustained reclaim work can therefore delay deletion/finalization cleanup and increase disk-retention/storage-exhaustion impact compared with the previous independent workers.

