## Report
Validated the finding as a logic/availability regression rather than a memory-safety crash. The commit introduces `BACKGROUND_DURABLE_CLEANUP_LIMIT: usize = 1` in `crates/server-core/src/coordinator/runtime.rs:50-52`, a process-local shared admission object in `runtime.rs:198-213`, and a non-blocking `try_acquire` that returns `None` with no queue/fairness when the class counter is at limit in `runtime.rs:119-143`. Both cleanup paths use the same `BackgroundWorkClass::DurableCleanup`: reclaim work at `runtime.rs:369-374` stores `pending_work` and sleeps `OBJECT_PAYLOAD_RECLAIM_PG_RETRY_COOLDOWN` on denial, while lifecycle sweeping at `runtime.rs:548-567` skips `run_lifecycle_sweep_at` on denial and then waits `LIFECYCLE_SWEEP_INTERVAL_MILLIS` before retrying; that interval is 1000 ms in `crates/server-core/src/coordinator.rs:208`. I added a targeted unit-test PoC using the real private `BackgroundWorkAdmission` implementation. The PoC holds the single DurableCleanup permit in a lifecycle-like path while a reclaim-like thread retries the same class. Command output: `shared durable_cleanup gate: reclaim-like worker saw 3 denied admissions over 300.463881ms before lifecycle-like permit was released` and the test passed. LLDB confirmed execution through the real vulnerable method: breakpoint at `server_core::coordinator::runtime::BackgroundWorkAdmission::try_acquire` with `class=DurableCleanup` at `runtime.rs:120`, called by the PoC test. Direct crash was not expected or observed because this is an availability/scheduling issue. Valgrind was attempted but is not installed in the container (`bash: command not found: valgrind`). This supports that the commit changed behavior beyond telemetry by serializing unrelated durable cleanup workloads through a single non-fair permit.

